  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1144 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nPrecip and Storm Chances...post frontal shower/sprinkle activity \nstarting to wane across the area late this evening, but lingering \nmoisture and weak lift could lead to some additional sprinkles \novernight in Monday morning. Shower activity will then focus \nacross MN and northwest WI, closer to a low pressure system and \nshortwave. However, isentropic lift and a ribbon on moisture will \nextend into parts of northeast WI. This will bring rain chances of\n30-75% to locations mainly north of Hwy 29 later Monday into \nTuesday morning, highest across north-central WI. The heavier and \nmost persistent rain will reside over far northwest WI, with \nlighter rain expected in our area, with probabilities of over \n0.25\" of rain through Tuesday night under 35%. Thermal profiles \nare showing a below freezing layer between 1000-2000 ft early \nTuesday, which if it materializes, it could be just cold enough \nfor a little sleet to mix in across far north-central WI. But \nthink temps will be slow to drop due to the expected cloud cover, \nand have opted to stick with just rain. The low-level dry air \nfeeding into the area on the northeast winds will eat away at the \neastward progression of the shower activity, but some light \nshowers or sprinkles will spread into parts of eastern WI at \ntimes. Another shortwave and frontal boundary will cross the area\non later Wednesday into early Thursday, bringing a 50-80% chance\nfor showers. A ribbon of mainly elevated instability will move \ninto the area late Wednesday (likely after sunset), bringing some \nlow thunder chances (~20%). Will continue to mention a slight \nchance for storms. No severe weather is expected.\n\nTemperatures...northeast/north flow behind the front will usher in\na much cooler day on Monday, with highs ranging from the upper 40s\nacross north-central WI to the mid 50s in the Fox Valley. The shot\nof cooler air will be short-lived, as highs will rebound 5-15 \ndegrees on Tuesday and back into the mid 60s to low 70s on \nWednesday. Above normal temps remain in place Thursday into next \nweekend, with highs mainly in upper 50s and 60s, with a 70 \npossible over the south on Thursday.",
